{
  "_comment": "Copy this file to secrets.local.json (same folder) and add one entry per server under 'profiles'. Shape: \"profiles\": { \"<profile name>\": \"<that server's rcon password>\" }. secrets.local.json is GITIGNORED and never committed. Keys are the profile NAMES from servers.local.json (see servers.local.json.example) - that file supplies host/port, this one supplies the password, and they are joined on the name, so renaming a profile in one file without the other means the panel shows the server but fails to authenticate. Values are that password as a plain string (<=23 chars - Plutonium silently ignores longer).",
  "_empty": "'profiles' ships EMPTY on purpose - copy this file as-is and do NOT add a placeholder entry. An ABSENT name is what lets the auto-seed below fill it in on first run, and what makes an unresolvable profile a loud 400 ('No rcon_password saved for server profile ...') instead of a silent one. A PRESENT name is a saved credential whatever its value: it suppresses the seed, reports to the panel as 'a password is saved', and is what goes on the wire.",
  "_blank": "So a blank value ('') means exactly one thing: this server genuinely has none - an explicit 'needs none', NOT 'unset'. Hand-write one only for such a server; the panel never stores a blank itself (saving an empty box DELETES the entry). Anywhere else a blank sends an unauthenticated packet, which Plutonium answers with nothing at all - leaving the panel to report 'Server not responding (timeout)', which is indistinguishable from a server that is down.",
  "_autoseed": "You usually do not write this file by hand. The panel seeds the LOOPBACK profile's password from dedicated.cfg the first time it starts (setup_rcon_vps.ps1 does the same on the VPS), and it never overwrites one already saved - blank included. For any other server, type the password into the panel once - it is written here over the loopback-only API.",
  "_privacy": "These values NEVER reach the browser. A panel request carries the profile NAME and server.js resolves the password locally, so it is not in a URL, not in a POST body, and not in the page. GET /api/secrets returns presence only ({ '<name>': true }).",
  "profiles": {}
}
